ill be on  low carbon energy rather than oil and  gas but is that enough so seppie bp's  profits may be hard to stomach for some  given  the the rising energy bills that people  are contending with not to mention the  climate crisis  but the company themselves argued that  they're trying to fund a hugely  expensive shift towards greener forms of  energy so  they they would say  leave us alone we need to make these  profits  well it appears that their plans towards  uh these uh more investment and  renewables were actually made before  uh the they understood the extent of  their profits uh for for this year so um  i'm not sure that their plans are  predicated on the profits that they've  made this year but also actually you  know we we've seen that they are  giving out uh you know money to their  shareholders they're they're paying that  money out and and it really you know  it's appealing for some to think that  actually well you know that money could  be used instead to alleviate this uh  cost of living crisis that so many in  the uk are facing  david what's your take on this do oil  companies need these big profits in  order to fund the transmission to  greener alternatives  well they're private companies to the  extent that they are participating in  this transition  they require some capital but i'm  skeptical that their motivations are as  pure as their um as their leaders  suggest i mean when you look at this  spending it is significant um i don't  want to say it's nothing but they're  still spending considerably more money  opening up new oil and gas capacity  which is in direct violation of what the  iea said was necessary um to allow the  world a chance of staying  to 1.5 degrees of warming they said that  meant no new investment in new oil and  gas infrastructure at all going forward  starting today um and bp is among the  best performing oil and gas companies  when it comes to the renewable  transition and yet still this they're  spending considerably more money on the  old dirty kinds of energy than on  building out their renewable portfolios  so i think we have to understand some of  the you know they are making a shift  here but the scale  of that shift is as important as the  fact of it and the scale is um according  to the iaea and the ipcc i'm still  woefully into fiction  sepi yet they would argue no doubt that  we still do need these fossil fuels  while we transition we still need gas in  this country for example  until there's enough renewable  infrastructure there to replace it  i mean i think that just just looking  again at the um the the the actions and  not words just wanted to highlight sorry  anna that the the investment in  renewables from bp actually halved  between 2018 and 2019 so i think i agree  with david that we need to see some more  um action and not just words and on your  point about you know the the  need for fossil fuels well that's that  is in the  ccc's pathways et cetera but it's vastly  reduced we know that we don't need any  new uh oil and gas but also we we know  that actually the ia who are not a  radical body by the way uh  they're very conservative actually have  said that it's not only that we  shouldn't go after the new oil and gases  that actually if uh countries listened  to their advice on the pace at which to  pursue renewables countries and  companies then we just wouldn't need uh  as much oil and gas uh  as you know these companies are  suggesting so i think we just need to be  quite careful you know we've already got  so much knowledge about the reserves  that are available that are extractable  we know that we need to keep much of  that in the ground anyway so it just  seems like throwing good money after bad  to pursue new investments here  and and david you're in the states at  the moment and there there are hearings  going on looking at various fossil fuel  industries um sustainability pledges and  examining them i mean how  much awareness public awareness is there  of what's going on and could this prove  to be some kind of watershed do you  think could it shift public opinion on  on what's going on  well i don't think these hearings in  particular are going to make mark a  dramatic break in public opinion but i  do think that we're living through a  relatively rapid cultural shift that is  um you know  meaning more and more people are  regarding and treating the fossil fuel  business as a kind of a villainous  entity in our in our lives and our world  um it's not the matter of one hearing um  or one climate summit or one  report but i do think that that shift is  moving forward i think that's one of the  main reasons why so many of these  companies are at least paying so much  lip service to the um to the green  transition they don't want to lose  a custom customer support and the  cultural cachet that they've had in the  past or at least don't want to lose it  as quickly as they might otherwise  otherwise lose it um but you know  whether or not what what that means in  terms of emissions under production i  think is is very much an open question  and there was a big new study that came  out yesterday wasn't just looking at oil  and gas companies but 25 major um  multinational corporations who had made  net zero pledges and found that actually  in aggregate those net zero pledges only  amounted to a reduction of about 40  percent of those companies emissions um  not 100 does the term net zero implies i  think we're seeing something quite  similar um across the economy maybe even  most especially in the fossil fuel  sector where companies are saying that  they're moving much faster than they  than they really are  okay interesting well let's move on to  our second topic right now because a  complete change in the way we produce  food could be one of the big solutions  to climate change not only could  regenerative farming help biodiversity  claims a new report from the world  wildlife fund but the emission savings  would be 